```json
{
  "name": "get_order_status",
  "description": "Look up the current status of ONE customer order by its order ID (format ORD-123456). Returns status, carrier, tracking number and last update time. Use this when the customer asks where an order is. Do not use it for refunds; use issue_refund.",
  "input_schema": {
    "type": "object",
    "properties": {
      "order_id": {"type": "string", "description": "Order ID like ORD-123456"}
    },
    "required": ["order_id"]
  }
}
```

The **description** is the most important lever. It should say what the tool does, when to use it (and when not to), what each parameter means and its format, what it returns, and its limits or side effects. Detailed descriptions beat terse ones.

Required fields are `name` (letters, digits, `_` and `-` only), `description` and `input_schema` (JSON Schema). Optional extras include `input_examples` (example inputs, which must validate against the schema or the request is rejected), `strict` (guarantees the call matches the schema) and `defer_loading` (lets tool search load the tool only when needed).
